1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 c5 3. Nf3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. d4 g6 6. Bd2 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Bg7 8. e4 Nb6 ({Not} 8... Bxd4 $2 9. Qa4+) ({but} 8... O-O {might be possible.}) 9. Be3 O-O 10. Be2 Nc6 {After this move White gets slightly the better pawn structure - but Black's position is very active.} 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. O-O Be6 13. Qc2 Qc8 14. Rfd1 c5 15. Rac1 Qb7 $5 {Black sacrifices a pawn for more activity.} 16. Bxc5 Rfc8 17. Be3 (17. b4 $5 {would be rather loosening, e.g.} a5 18. Qb1 (18. Bxb6 axb4) 18... Nd7) ({Perhaps White should try} 17. Bxb6 {to keep his pawn or 17 Bd4(!) but in any case Black should retain plenty of chances.}) 17... Bxa2 {In fact White had missed this.} 18. b3 Rxc3 19. Qxa2 Rxc1 20. Rxc1 Rc8 21. Rxc8+ Nxc8 22. Qa4 {White has the advantage since has the bishop pair in a position where there is still play on both sides of the board.} Kf8 23. Ba6 Qc7 24. Qc4 Qxc4 25. bxc4 Nb6 26. c5 Na8 27. c6 Nc7 28. Bc4 a6 29. f4 (29. Bf4 $5 {would force} e5) 29... Ke8 30. Kf2 e6 31. e5 Bf8 32. Ke2 Bb4 33. Kd3 Kd8 34. Bb6 Kc8 35. Kc2 a5 36. Kb3 Bd2 37. g3 ({"Here my bulletin gives} 37. Ka4 {allowing} Be1 (37... Bxf4 {. "This I do not believe as White had played g3 in the adjourned position. I shall continue to give the score as in the bulletin, but please bear in mind that White's g3 was left out." (Unattributed comment in International Chess No.1 (February 1979) - probably one of the two players? Whoever it was continues giving the moves...}) 38. Bxa5 Bxa5 39. Kxa5 Na8 40. Kb5 Kc7 41. Kc5 Nb6 42. Bb3 Nc8 43. Bc2 Ne7 {"Black has just sealed. During his adjournment analysis Christiansen decided rightly or wrongly, that he must prevent ...h5 at all costs. He therefore played the awful} 44. g4 $4 {(JS note - now the two versions of the score finally merge)}) 37... Be1 38. Ka4 Bd2 39. Bxa5 Bxa5 40. Kxa5 Na8 41. Kb5 Kc7 42. Kc5 Nb6 43. Bb3 Nc8 44. Bc2 Ne7 45. g4 Ng8 $4 ({"Black should not play} 45... Nxc6 46. Ba4 Nd8 ({Stockfish 17:} 46... Ne7 47. Be8 Nd5 $11) 47. Be8) ({"but he has} 45... Nd5 $1 {and if} 46. f5 {then} Ne3 {- Black will emerge with an extra pawn!"}) 46. Be4 Ne7 47. h3 Ng8 48. h4 $2 ({"White should have played the immediate} 48. f5 gxf5 49. gxf5 exf5 50. Bxf5 h6 51. h4 Ne7 52. Be4 Ng8 53. h5 $1 Ne7 54. Bd5 {zugzwang - this is the position White is aiming for."}) 48... Ne7 (48... Nh6 $1 {would render it very hard for White to force through f5.}) 49. Bf3 (49. f5 $1) 49... Ng8 50. h5 Ne7 (50... Nh6 $1) 51. Be4 gxh5 (51... Ng8 52. f5) 52. gxh5 h6 53. Kb5 Nc8 54. Kc5 Na7 55. Bg2 Nc8 56. Bf3 Ne7 ({Stockfish 17:} 56... Nb6 $11) 57. Be4 Ng8 58. f5 exf5 59. Bxf5 Ne7 60. Be4 Nc8 (60... Ng8 61. Bg2 Ne7 62. Bd5 {zugzwang}) 61. Bf3 Nb6 62. Bd1 Nc8 63. Bb3 Nb6 64. Bxf7 Na4+ 65. Kd4 Nb6 66. Be8 Nc8 67. Bd7 Nb6 68. Ke4 Kd8 69. Kf5 Ke7 70. Be6 {Source: International Chess No.1 (February 1979), with comments by one of the players (not clear which one). The score has been amended to incorporate an extra pair of moves suggested by the original annotator. JS} 1-0

